paul.dev: Running the sample-app during the onboarding workshop and hit a weird streaming bug on flaky mobile network — chunks arrive out of order and the client ends up with duplicated final tokens after reconnect.

paul.dev: Minimal repro: open /demo, start streaming chat, toggle airplane mode then reconnect. UI shows garbled JSON and sometimes two [DONE]s.

paul.dev: Relevant fetch/EventSource code in the sample:

paul.dev: ```js
const es = new EventSource('/v1/streams?session=abc');
es.onmessage = (e) => {
  // we expect e.data to be JSON fragments, we parse and append
  const obj = JSON.parse(e.data);
  appendToken(obj.token);
};
```

alex.m: ugh. iirc EventSource uses text/event-stream framing and can split long lines across packets. If the client parses each e.data as standalone JSON that can blow up.

maria: can you paste raw logs? especially parser debug with seq ids and any `id:` fields from server.

paul.dev: logs attached (snip):

paul.dev: ```
[stream-parser] conn=42 pkt=1 data="{\"token\":\"Hello \""
[stream-parser] conn=42 pkt=2 data="World\"}"
[stream-parser] conn=42 pkt=3 data="{\"token\":\"!\"}"
[client] reconnect after 2s, last-id=none
[stream-parser] conn=43 pkt=1 data="{\"token\":\"Hello \""
[stream-parser] conn=43 pkt=2 data="World\"}"
[client] append: Hello 
[client] append: World}
[client] append: !
[client] final: Hello World} !
```

paul.dev: you can see the middle packet ended with a bare `}` chunk which broke the client json assembly and left an extra brace. then on reconnect partial state caused duplication.

jonah: two separate issues: 1) parser should buffer until a complete JSON object is received (or use application/json-stream framing like newline-delimited), 2) reconnection/resume semantics need an `id:`/Last-Event-ID to avoid re-appending partial work.

alex.m: Node sample-app uses the browser EventSource polyfill for SSR + client hydration parity. The polyfill splits streaming messages differently than native browsers. Python SDK sample uses a streaming-response iterator which concatenates chunks before parse -> that's probably why python sample didn't show it.

maria: so SDK parity gap noted — Node/browser path is optimistic-parsing, python is conservative-buffering. we should normalise. Also onboarding workshop uses the optimistic pattern in sample-app which is bad.

eng-runtime: @paul.dev thanks for the repro. tagging eng-runtime to triage. Quick questions: can you confirm server is sending `id:` lines? also are we including any `seq` meta per token?

paul.dev: server currently sends per-message metadata as a small prefix in the JSON (e.g., {\"seq\":12, \"token\":\"...\"}) but not SSE `id:`. We can probably add SSE-level ids if needed.

eng-runtime: plan: (1) push a quick sample-app patch to buffer until a full JSON object is recognised (safe fallback) — will do canary today, (2) add optional SSE `id:` header from runtime for resumable streams, (3) add guidance in devex docs: "use newline-delimited or explicit SSE ids for reliable client resumability".

alex.m: for immediate workshop workaround: switch sample-app to use fetch readableStream and manual line-assembly (example below). it's slightly longer but more robust across polyfills: 

alex.m: ```js
const r = await fetch('/v1/streams?session=abc');
const reader = r.body.getReader();
let buf = '';
while(true){
  const {done, value} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += new TextDecoder().decode(value);
  let idx;
  while((idx = buf.indexOf('\n')) >= 0){
    const line = buf.slice(0, idx).trim();
    buf = buf.slice(idx+1);
    if(!line) continue;
    // parse line which should be a complete JSON fragment
    appendToken(JSON.parse(line).token);
  }
}
```

maria: ^ that's a good short-term fix. we'll also update sample-app README with notes about flaky networks and polyfill differences.

jonah: also be careful with automatic retry logic — if you retry the entire stream after a partial append you will duplicate. need idempotency or server-side resumability (Last-Event-ID + server replay from that seq).

eng-runtime: opened ENG-STREAM-512 to track: sample-app patch, runtime SSE id flag, docs. assigning to @alex.m for sample-app patch and @eng-runtime for runtime change. ETA: canary tomorrow for sample-app, runtime change in next sprint.

paul.dev: awesome, thanks. i'll push a temporary branch to the workshop repo using alex's reader approach and call it out in the slides. :thumbsup:

